---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const pharmaCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Pharmacokinetics: what the body does to the drug', note: 'ADME — Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism, Excretion. Half-life sets the dosing interval: after ~5 half-lives, a drug reaches steady state. Miss a dose schedule and the level never stabilises.' },
    { math: '2 · Pharmacodynamics: what the drug does to the body', note: 'Receptors, dose–response curves, therapeutic window. Drugs are keys; the fit decides effect and side effects — most "side effects" are the same key fitting other locks.' },
    { math: '3 · The therapeutic window', note: 'The gap between effective and toxic dose. Narrow-window drugs (warfarin, lithium, digoxin) need blood-level monitoring; wide-window drugs (most antibiotics) forgive dosing errors.' },
    { math: '4 · Interactions: the polypharmacy web', note: 'One drug changes another\'s metabolism (liver enzymes, grapefruit juice inhibiting CYP3A4). Elderly patients on 10+ medications are chemistry experiments — medication review is a clinical skill.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/medicine/intermediate/pharmacology-basics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Pharmacology is the science of <strong>how drugs and bodies negotiate</strong>: what the body
    does to the drug (kinetics) and what the drug does to the body (dynamics). Nearly every
    prescribing decision — dose, interval, combination — follows from four ADME facts and one
    receptor curve.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the ADME framework, the therapeutic window, and the interaction web" widget="equationStep" config={pharmaCfg} />

  <h2>How drugs are discovered and proven</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>From molecule to medicine:</strong> target identification → screening → animal and lab testing → Phase I (safety, healthy volunteers) → II (does it work?) → III (does it beat standard care, thousands of patients?) → approval → Phase IV surveillance. ~10–15 years, ~$1–2B, ~90% attrition.</li>
    <li><strong>Aspirin's story is the classic:</strong> salicylates from willow bark (folk medicine) → acetylated for stomach tolerance (Bayer, 1899) → mechanism discovered decades later (prostaglandins, Nobel 1982) → new uses still emerging (heart attack prevention).</li>
    <li><strong>Antibiotic stewardship:</strong> using antibiotics Carelessly breeds resistance — the pharmacology lesson meets the infection lesson at the prescriber's pen.</li>
    <li><strong>Placebos are real:</strong> expectation measurably changes symptoms (and brain chemistry). Blinding in trials exists to separate the drug's effect from the ritual's — the psychology ladder connects.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Rang & Dale's Pharmacology; BMJ and FDA drug approval pipeline data; Vane, Nobel lecture (1982).</p>
</TopicPage>
